<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sigmaringen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sigmaringen is a town on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, dominated by a castle on a rock above the river. Granted to the counts of Hohenzollern by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in the 1530s, it became the seat of the Catholic Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen line, whose members later became kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. In 1944–1945 its castle housed the exiled leaders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sigmaringen.jpg" alt="Sigmaringen" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Felix König · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sigmaringen_August_2015.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
